    "thm-tdmath-compact-continuous-mixture-attainment": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $K$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. The **convex hull** $\\operatorname{conv}K$ is the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$. A **phase generator** is a point $y\\in K$ with declared energy $a(y)$. A **mixture** is a Borel probability measure $P$ on $K$. Its barycenter and mean energy are the coordinatewise Lebesgue integrals\n$$\\bar y(P):=\\int_K y\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\bar a(P):=\\int_K a(y)\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nThese integrals are finite because each coordinate and $a$ are bounded on compact $K$. For prescribed mean $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, the **mixture energy** is\n$$a_{\\rm mix}(x):=\\inf\\{\\bar a(P):P\\text{ is a Borel probability on }K,\\ \\bar y(P)=x\\}.$$\nThe admissible set is nonempty since every finite convex combination of points of $K$ is the barycenter of a finite atomic probability. The definition does not assert that the infimum is attained; the theorem on this page gives a compact continuous sufficient condition.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.2",
            "5.3",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-borel-probability-law-on-a-polish-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let S be Polish in the sense of [[def-polish-space]]. A **Borel probability law on S** is a countably additive measure on $\\mathcal B(S)$ with total mass one. Here $\\mathcal B(S)$ is [[def-borel-sigma-algebra]] and probability measure means [[def-probability-measure]]. A compatible complete metric may be fixed for a construction; it is not additional data in the law. The empty space admits no such law, since its measure must be both zero and one.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Define $\\Phi(y)=(y,a(y))\\in\\mathbb R^{d+1}$ and $G=\\Phi(K)$. The map $\\Phi$ is continuous. Each coordinate of $\\Phi$ is continuous on compact $K$, so [F4] bounds it; hence $G$ is bounded. To see $G$ is closed, take $(y,r)\\notin G$. If $y\\notin K$, closedness of $K$ gives a neighborhood of $y$ disjoint from $K$. If $y\\in K$, then $r\\ne a(y)$, and continuity of $a$ gives neighborhoods of $y$ and $r$ whose product misses the graph of $a$. Thus the complement of $G$ is open. By [F3], $G$ is compact.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In a finite convex combination, discard zero-weight terms. If more than $d+2$ positive-weight points $g_i\\in G\\subset\\mathbb R^{d+1}$ remain, the vectors $(g_i,1)\\in\\mathbb R^{d+2}$ are linearly dependent; hence there are coefficients $c_i$, not all zero, with $\\sum_i c_i=0$ and $\\sum_i c_i g_i=0$. Both signs occur. Replacing the positive weights $\\theta_i$ by $\\theta_i-tc_i$, where $t=\\min_{c_i>0}\\theta_i/c_i$, preserves their sum and combination, keeps them nonnegative, and makes at least one zero. Repeating gives at most $d+2$ points. Pad shorter combinations by zero weights and repeat any fixed point of nonempty $G$. The parameter set $G^{d+2}\\times\\Delta_{d+2}$ is closed and bounded in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space, hence compact by [F3]. The map $\\Theta:G^{d+2}\\times\\Delta_{d+2}\\to\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, $\\Theta((g_i),\\theta)=\\sum_i\\theta_i g_i$, is continuous and has image exactly $\\operatorname{conv}G$: every open cover of the image pulls back to an open cover of the compact parameter set and therefore has a finite subcover. Thus $\\operatorname{conv}G$ is compact and closed by [F3].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$. Its fiber $F_x=\\{r:(x,r)\\in\\operatorname{conv}G\\}$ is nonempty, since each finite convex combination of generators gives such a point. It is closed and bounded because $\\operatorname{conv}G$ is closed and bounded by [F3]; hence it is compact by [F3]. Therefore [F4] gives a minimum $r_x$. A convex-combination representation of $(x,r_x)$ by points $(y_i,a(y_i))$ defines a finite atomic Borel probability $P_x$ with barycenter $x$ and energy $r_x$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Conversely, for any Borel probability $P$ on $K$, the vector-valued map $\\Phi$ is bounded and coordinatewise integrable by [F2]. Its integral is in $\\operatorname{conv}G$: for each $\\epsilon>0$, cover compact $G$ by finitely many open Euclidean balls of radius $\\epsilon/2$ and pull the cover back to $K$ by $\\Phi$. Disjointifying the finite preimages in order gives a finite Borel partition of $K$ whose pieces have image diameter at most $\\epsilon$. Choose a point in each nonempty piece and weight it by that piece's $P$-mass. The resulting finite convex combination differs coordinatewise from $\\int\\Phi\\,dP$ by at most $\\epsilon$; as $\\operatorname{conv}G$ is closed by step 2.1, the integral belongs to it. If $\\bar y(P)=x$, then $(x,\\bar a(P))\\in\\operatorname{conv}G$, so $\\bar a(P)\\ge r_x$. Together with step 3.1 this proves that the all-measure infimum equals $r_x$ and is attained by $P_x$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "To prove lower semicontinuity, let $x_n\\to x$ in $\\operatorname{conv}K$ and put $L=\\liminf_n a_{\\rm mix}(x_n)$. The values lie between $\\min_K a$ and $\\max_K a$, attained by [F4], so $L$ is finite. By the definition of liminf, choose a subsequence $n_j$ with $a_{\\rm mix}(x_{n_j})\\to L$. Each pair $(x_{n_j},a_{\\rm mix}(x_{n_j}))$ lies in the closed set $\\operatorname{conv}G$ by step 4.1, and the pairs converge to $(x,L)$; hence $(x,L)\\in\\operatorname{conv}G$. The minimum in the fiber at $x$ satisfies $a_{\\rm mix}(x)\\le L=\\liminf_n a_{\\rm mix}(x_n)$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-2",
          "claim": "Combine minimizers $P_x$ and $P_z$ with weights $s$ and $1-s$, $0\\le s\\le1$. The combined finite measure has barycenter $sx+(1-s)z$ and energy $s a_{\\rm mix}(x)+(1-s)a_{\\rm mix}(z)$. By step 4.1, the minimum at the combined barycenter is no greater than this value. Hence $a_{\\rm mix}$ is convex.",
          "step": "5.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-3",
          "claim": "The support reduction in 2.1 first gives at most $d+2$ atoms. If an optimal representation has more than $d+1$ positive weights, the points $y_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$ have an affine dependence $\\sum_i c_i y_i=0$, $\\sum_i c_i=0$. For sufficiently small positive $t$, both weight lists $\\theta_i\\pm t c_i$ remain nonnegative and preserve the barycenter. If $\\sum_i c_i a(y_i)\\ne0$, one of these lists strictly lowers energy, contradicting optimality; hence this sum is zero. Increase $t$ in one sign to the first value at which a positive coefficient becomes zero. The new weights preserve barycenter and energy and still attain the minimum; iteration leaves at most $d+1$ positive weights.",
          "step": "5.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Finally, let $g$ be any convex function on $\\operatorname{conv}K$ with $g(y)\\le a(y)$ for $y\\in K$. For the finite optimal measure from 4.1, finite Jensen (obtained by iterating the two-point convexity inequality) gives $g(x)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i g(y_i)\\le\\sum_i\\theta_i a(y_i)=a_{\\rm mix}(x)$. Since $a_{\\rm mix}(y)\\le a(y)$ for $y\\in K$ by the point mass at $y$, it is itself such a convex minorant and dominates every other one.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 5.2",
            "step 5.3",
            "algebra",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The nonempty generator hypothesis is used to construct G and a fiber minimizer ."
        },
