    "thm-tdmath-independent-microcanonical-block-limit": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-finite-site-shell-and-canonical-law",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite alphabet $A=\\{1,\\ldots,q\\}$, $q\\ge1$, and real single-site energies $e_1,\\ldots,e_q$ in a fixed energy unit. At inverse parameter $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$Z_1(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^q e^{-\\beta e_j},\\qquad p_j(\\beta):=e^{-\\beta e_j}/Z_1(\\beta),\\qquad E(\\beta):=\\sum_{j=1}^q p_j(\\beta)e_j.$$\nFor $N\\ge1$, a configuration $i=(i_1,\\ldots,i_N)\\in A^N$ has additive energy $H_N(i):=\\sum_{r=1}^Ne_{i_r}$. Given a width $\\delta_N\\ge0$, the **shell** is\n$$\\Gamma_N(\\beta,\\delta_N):=\\{i\\in A^N:|H_N(i)-NE(\\beta)|\\le\\delta_N\\}.$$\nWhen this finite set is nonempty, its microcanonical law means the uniform probability on $\\Gamma_N$, and its dimensionless entropy is $\\log|\\Gamma_N|$. A type is a vector $t=(n_1/N,\\ldots,n_q/N)$, where the nonnegative integer counts sum to $N$; its multiplicity is $N!/\\prod_j n_j!$.\n\nThe convention fixes both the shell center and the law within the shell. It makes no physical claim that an apparatus has resolution $\\delta_N$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-sum",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Throughout this page $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), in particular an ordered field\n([[def-ordered-field]]) and a field ([[def-field]]), and $\\mathbb{N}$ is the set\nof natural numbers ([[def-natural-numbers]]) with successor $\\sigma(n) = n + 1$\n([[def-nat-addition]]).\n\nLet $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a sequence of reals, written $a_k$ for\n$a(k)$. **Finite sums and finite products of $a$ are defined by recursion on the\nupper index**, which is legitimate because of the recursion theorem\n([[thm-recursion]]). That theorem produces a function of one variable, so the\nrunning index has to be carried along inside the value: applying it to the set\n$A = \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}$, the starting element $(0, 0)$ and the function\n$f(n, s) = (\\sigma(n),\\, s + a_n)$ gives a unique\n$g : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}$ with\n\n$$g(0) = (0, 0), \\qquad g(\\sigma(n)) = f(g(n)) \\quad (n \\in \\mathbb{N}).$$\n\nWrite $g(n) = \\big(\\pi_1(g(n)),\\, \\Sigma_n\\big)$ for its two coordinates.\n\n**The first coordinate is the index itself, and that is a small induction, not an\nobservation** ([[thm-induction-principle]]). Indeed $\\pi_1(g(0)) = 0$; and if\n$\\pi_1(g(n)) = n$, then\n$g(\\sigma(n)) = f\\big(\\pi_1(g(n)), \\Sigma_n\\big) = \\big(\\sigma(\\pi_1(g(n))),\\, \\Sigma_n + a_{\\pi_1(g(n))}\\big) = \\big(\\sigma(n),\\, \\Sigma_n + a_n\\big)$,\nso $\\pi_1(g(\\sigma(n))) = \\sigma(n)$. By induction $\\pi_1(g(n)) = n$ for every\n$n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Only now may the second coordinate of the two displayed\nclauses be read off, and doing so gives\n\n$$\\Sigma_0 = 0, \\qquad \\Sigma_{\\sigma(n)} = \\Sigma_n + a_n .$$\n\n$\\Sigma$ is moreover the **unique** function $\\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with those\ntwo properties: if $\\Sigma'$ also has them then $n \\mapsto (n, \\Sigma'_n)$ satisfies\nthe two clauses defining $g$, hence equals $g$ by the uniqueness clause of\n[[thm-recursion]], so $\\Sigma' = \\Sigma$.\n\nWe write $\\sum_{k < n} a_k := \\Sigma_n$. The same construction with starting\nelement $(0, 1)$ and $f(n, p) = (\\sigma(n),\\, p \\cdot a_n)$, with the same\ninduction on the first coordinate and the same uniqueness argument, gives the\nunique $\\Pi : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n\n$$\\Pi_0 = 1, \\qquad \\Pi_{\\sigma(n)} = \\Pi_n \\cdot a_n ,$$\n\nand we write $\\prod_{k < n} a_k := \\Pi_n$.\n\n**Notation.** For $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ we abbreviate\n\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n} a_k := \\sum_{k < n+1} a_k, \\qquad \\prod_{k=0}^{n} a_k := \\prod_{k < n+1} a_k,$$\n\nand, for a general lower index $m$ with $m \\le n + 1$, writing $d = n + 1 - m$\nfor the number of terms,\n\n$$\\sum_{k=m}^{n} a_k := \\sum_{j < d} a_{m+j}, \\qquad \\prod_{k=m}^{n} a_k := \\prod_{j < d} a_{m+j} .$$\n\nWhen $m = n + 1$ we have $d = 0$ and the sum is **empty**, with value $0$, while\nthe empty product has value $1$. In the same spirit $\\sum_{k=0}^{-1} a_k$ is\nnotation for the empty sum $\\Sigma_0 = 0$ and $\\prod_{k=0}^{-1} a_k$ for the\nempty product $\\Pi_0 = 1$; the index $-1$ never occurs as an element of\n$\\mathbb{N}$ and is only a way of writing \"no terms\".\n\nOnly finitely many values of $a$ enter $\\sum_{k<n} a_k$, so the notation\n$\\sum_{k<n} a_k$ and $\\prod_{k<n} a_k$ is also used for a list\n$a_0, \\dots, a_{n-1}$ of reals given without reference to any extension of the\nlist to all of $\\mathbb{N}$: extend the list by $a_k = 0$ (respectively\n$a_k = 1$) for $k \\ge n$ and apply the definition above.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-real-exponential-function-and-e",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x\\in\\mathbb R$, define\n$$\\exp(x):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^n}{\\iota(n!)}, \\qquad e:=\\exp(1),$$\nprovided by the all-real convergence proved in [[lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius]]. Here $n!\\in\\mathbb N$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], $\\iota(n!)$ is its nonzero real image ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), and powers and series are those of [[def-integer-power]] and [[def-series]].\n\nThis is a real power series centred at $0$ ([[def-real-power-series-and-radius-of-convergence]]). No logarithm, irrational power, or differential equation enters the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a type $t_j=n_j/N$, the number of configurations of that type is $M(n)=N!/\\prod_j n_j!$. The increasing function $\\log x$ gives $$\\int_1^m\\log x\\,dx\\le\\log(m!)\\le\\int_1^{m+1}\\log x\\,dx\\quad(m\\ge1).$$ Evaluating the antiderivative $x\\log x-x$ and using $\\log(1+1/m)\\le1/m$ shows $\\log(m!)=m\\log m-m+O(\\log(m+1)+1)$ with an absolute constant; the case $m=0$ uses $0!=1$ and $0\\log0=0$. Subtracting the $q$ denominator estimates from the numerator estimate gives a constant $C_q$ such that $$NH(t)-C_q\\log(N+1)-C_q\\le\\log M(n)\\le NH(t)+C_q\\log(N+1)+C_q,$$ where $H(t)=-\\sum_jt_j\\log t_j$. The sharper upper bound $M(n)\\le e^{NH(t)}$ follows by expanding $1=(\\sum_jt_j)^N$ and retaining the nonnegative terms with counts $n$. For later limits, [F4] gives $0\\le\\log x=\\int_1^xdt/t\\le2\\sqrt{x}$ for $x\\ge1$, hence $\\log(N+1)/N\\le2\\sqrt{N+1}/N\\to0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For any probability vector $t$, define $D(t\\Vert p)=\\sum_{j:t_j>0}t_j\\log(t_j/p_j)$. From [F4], $\\log u\\ge1-1/u$ for $u>0$, with equality only at $u=1$: apply $\\log z\\le z-1$ to $z=1/u$ and negate. Therefore $$D(t\\Vert p)\\ge\\sum_{j:t_j>0}(t_j-p_j)=1-\\sum_{j:t_j>0}p_j\\ge0.$$ Since every $p_j>0$, equality forces every index into the support and $t_j/p_j=1$ for each $j$, so $t=p$. Also $\\log p_j=-\\beta e_j-\\log Z_1$, hence $D(t\\Vert p)=-H(t)+\\beta\\sum_jt_je_j+\\log Z_1$. In particular, among types of exact mean energy $E$, entropy is at most $H(p)$, with equality only at $p$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Round down $Np_j$ for $j<q$ and put the remainder in $n_q$. Then $|n_j/N-p_j|\\le1/N$ for $j<q$ and $|n_q/N-p_q|\\le(q-1)/N$. Thus $$\\left|\\sum_jn_je_j-NE\\right|\\le\\sum_{j<q}|e_j|+(q-1)|e_q|\\le q\\sum_j|e_j|\\le\\delta_N.$$ This type belongs to $\\Gamma_N$, proving nonemptiness. Its frequencies tend to $p$. For $0<x\\le1$, [F4] gives $$0\\le-x\\log x=x\\log(1/x)\\le2\\sqrt{x},$$ by integrating $1/t\\le1/\\sqrt t$ on $[1,1/x]$; hence $-x\\log x$ extends continuously by $0$ at $x=0$ and $H(t)\\to H(p)$. By the lower bound in 1.1, the shell contains at least $\\exp(NH(p)-o(N))$ configurations.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\epsilon>0$ and let $\\Delta_q=\\{t\\in[0,1]^q:\\sum_jt_j=1\\}$ and $C=\\{t\\in\\Delta_q:|t-p|\\ge\\epsilon\\}$. The simplex is closed and bounded, and $C$ is closed in it, so both are compact by [F5]. If $C=\\emptyset$, the claim is vacuous. Otherwise let $F=\\{t\\in C:\\sum_jt_je_j=E\\}$, a closed subset of $C$. If $F=\\emptyset$, the continuous function $t\\mapsto|\\sum_jt_je_j-E|$ has a positive minimum on $C$ by [F6], so choose $\\tau$ smaller than this minimum and any $\\eta>0$. If $F\\ne\\emptyset$, [F6] gives a maximum $h_F$ of $H$ on $F$; step 1.2 gives $h_F<H(p)$. Put $\\eta=(H(p)-h_F)/2$ and let $U=\\{t\\in C:H(t)<H(p)-\\eta\\}$, a relative open neighborhood of $F$. The compact set $C\\setminus U$, if nonempty, has a positive minimum $m$ of $|\\sum_jt_je_j-E|$ by [F6]; choose $0<\\tau<m$. If $C\\setminus U=\\emptyset$, choose any $\\tau>0$. In either case, every $t\\in C$ with $|\\sum_jt_je_j-E|\\le\\tau$ satisfies $H(t)\\le H(p)-\\eta$. For large $N$, $\\delta_N/N\\le\\tau$; therefore every shell type outside the $\\epsilon$-ball has entropy at most $H(p)-\\eta$. There are at most $(N+1)^q$ types, and 1.1 bounds the number of configurations of each by $\\exp[NH(t)]$. By the log-growth estimate in 1.1, this type-count factor is subexponential, so division by the shell lower bound in step 2.1 shows that the shell probability outside the ball tends to zero exponentially. Thus the random type converges in probability to $p$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Conditional on a type $t$, a uniform configuration is a uniformly ordered list of its $N$ labeled positions. Couple the first $k$ draws without replacement with $k$ independent uniform position draws: if the independent positions are distinct, keep them; if not, replace them by an independently chosen uniform ordered distinct $k$-tuple. The resulting tuple is uniform among distinct positions and agrees with the original draws except on the collision event. A union bound over the $\\binom{k}{2}$ pairs gives collision probability at most $k(k-1)/(2N)$. Hence the type-conditional label marginal differs in total variation from $t^{\\otimes k}$ by at most this quantity. Since $t\\to p$ in probability, for each fixed tuple $(i_1,\\ldots,i_k)\\in A^k$ the random variable $X_N=\\left|\\prod_{r=1}^kt_{i_r}-\\prod_{r=1}^kp_{i_r}\\right|$ tends to zero in probability and is bounded by $1$. For every $\\epsilon>0$, $\\mathbb E X_N\\le\\epsilon+P(X_N>\\epsilon)$, so $\\mathbb E X_N\\to0$; summing over the finite set $A^k$ proves the marginal converges to $p^{\\otimes k}$ in total variation.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "The rounded shell type and lower bound in step 2.1 give $\\liminf_NN^{-1}\\log|\\Gamma_N|\\ge H(p)$. For any shell type $t$, step 1.2 gives the exact identity\n$$H(t)=H(p)+\\beta\\left(\\sum_jt_je_j-E\\right)-D(t\\Vert p)\\le H(p)+|\\beta|\\delta_N/N.$$\nThere are at most $(N+1)^q$ types, and step 1.1 bounds each multiplicity by $e^{NH(t)}$. Therefore\n$$|\\Gamma_N|\\le(N+1)^q\\exp\\!\\left(NH(p)+|\\beta|\\delta_N\\right).$$\nBy the log-growth estimate in 1.1, $q\\log(N+1)/N\\to0$. Since also $\\delta_N/N\\to0$, the limsup is at most $H(p)$. Together with the lower bound this proves the entropy-density limit, including $\\beta=0$, without invoking a nonexistent compactness step.",
          "step": "5.1",
