    "thm-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-model": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,(\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0},P)$ carry a standard Brownian motion $B$ adapted to the filtration, with future increments independent of the present sigma-algebra. Fix constants $\\gamma>0$ and $\\sigma>0$, and an $\\mathcal F_0$-measurable real random variable $X_0$ with finite second moment. A **continuous adapted solution of the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck equation** on $[0,T]$ is a continuous adapted process $X$ satisfying, almost surely for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n$$X_t=X_0-\\gamma\\int_0^tX_s\\,ds+\\sigma B_t,$$\nwhere the time integral is the ordinary Lebesgue integral along the continuous path. In differential notation this is $dX_t=-\\gamma X_t\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dB_t$, with the stochastic differential interpreted through the displayed integral equation.\n\nThe definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. The Itô integral in the explicit representation is the square-integrable predictable-process integral of [[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-brownian-motion",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. A real process\n$B=(B_t)_{t\\ge0}$ is a **standard Brownian motion** if:\n\n1. $B_0=0$ almost surely;\n2. for every finite list $0=t_0<t_1<\\cdots<t_n$, the increments\n   $B_{t_j}-B_{t_{j-1}}$ are mutually independent and have laws\n   $N(0,t_j-t_{j-1})$; and\n3. there is one measurable event $A$ with $P(A)=1$ such that\n   $t\\mapsto B_t(\\omega)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ for every\n   $\\omega\\in A$.\n\nBy\n[[lem-brownian-gaussian-covariance-is-equivalent-to-independent-stationary-normal-increments]],\nthe first two clauses are equivalent to saying that $B$ is a centered Gaussian\nprocess with covariance $\\min(s,t)$. Clause 3 is additional: it cannot be\nrecovered from finite-dimensional distributions alone.\n\nNo filtration is part of this definition. In particular, no completed or\nright-continuous filtration and no Markov or martingale assertion is silently\nimposed. Modifications and indistinguishability retain the distinct meanings in\n[[def-law-modification-and-indistinguishability-of-processes]]. The cases\n$n=0$, $t_1=0$, and zero-length increments are respectively vacuous or already\ncovered by $B_0=0$; the increment list itself is strictly increasing.\n\nChoice is declared because the normal-law and Gaussian/increment-equivalence\ninterfaces construct and identify normal laws under AC. The continuity clause\nselects no path and makes no additional use of choice.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-existence-of-continuous-brownian-motion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. The canonical centered Gaussian coordinate process\nwith covariance $E[X_sX_t]=\\min(s,t)$ has a continuous modification $B$, and\n$B$ is a standard Brownian motion. In particular, standard Brownian motion\nexists.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-elementary-predictable-brownian-integrand",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]], and fix a probability space\n$(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$ with a continuous-time filtration\n$(\\mathcal F_t)_{t\\ge0}$\n[[def-continuous-time-filtration-and-all-pairs-martingale]] and a standard\nBrownian motion $B$ [[def-brownian-motion]] on it. **Standing hypothesis\n(H).** $B$ is adapted to $(\\mathcal F_t)$ and for all $0\\le s<t$ the increment\n$B_t-B_s$ is independent of $\\mathcal F_s$ and has law $N(0,t-s)$. The raw\nnatural filtration and the usual augmented natural filtration\n[[def-natural-and-usual-augmented-brownian-filtrations]] both satisfy (H) for a\nstandard Brownian motion, by\n[[thm-brownian-future-path-markov-property]]; for a general filtration, (H) is\npart of the data and is not automatic. Every statement in this development\nnames (H) when it is used.\n\nThroughout, fix a finite horizon $T>0$. An **elementary predictable Brownian\nintegrand** on $[0,T]$ is a process of the form\n$$H_s(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\\xi_k(\\omega)\\,1_{(t_k,t_{k+1}]}(s),\\qquad s\\in[0,T],$$\nwhere $0=t_0<t_1<\\cdots<t_m=T$ is a finite partition of $[0,T]$, each\n$\\xi_k$ is a bounded real $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$-measurable random variable, and\nthe values at the partition points are irrelevant because the intervals are\nleft-open and right-closed. The **mesh** of the representation is\n$\\max_k(t_{k+1}-t_k)$, and the **supremum norm** of the representation is\n$\\max_k\\|\\xi_k\\|_\\infty$. The integrand itself is the process $H$; a second\nlist of the same name with different coefficients is the same elementary\nintegrand only if the two processes coincide in the almost-everywhere sense\nmade precise below.\n\nThe following properties are part of the definition and are used at once.\n\n1. **Predictability.** $H$ is predictable\n   [[def-progressively-measurable-and-predictable-process]]. Indeed, for a\n   Borel set $\\Gamma\\subseteq\\mathbb R$,\n   $$\\{H\\in\\Gamma\\}=Z_\\Gamma\\cup\\bigcup_{k=0}^{m-1}\\bigl((t_k,t_{k+1}]\\times\\{\\xi_k\\in\\Gamma\\}\\bigr), \\qquad Z_\\Gamma:=\\begin{cases}\\{0\\}\\times\\Omega,&0\\in\\Gamma,\\\\\\varnothing,&0\\notin\\Gamma,\\end{cases}$$\n   a finite union of generators of the predictable sigma-algebra because\n   $\\{\\xi_k\\in\\Gamma\\}\\in\\mathcal F_{t_k}$ and\n   $\\{0\\}\\times\\Omega$ is a time-zero generator. Consequently $H$ is progressively\n   measurable and measurable for the product sigma-algebra\n   $\\mathcal B([0,T])\\otimes\\mathcal F$, and $H$ belongs to\n   $L^2([0,T]\\times\\Omega,\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)$: with\n   $c:=\\max_k\\|\\xi_k\\|_\\infty<\\infty$ one has\n   $E\\int_0^TH_s^2\\,ds\\le c^2T<\\infty$.\n\n2. **Endpoint and null-set conventions.** Replacing the intervals by\n   $(t_k,t_{k+1}]$ makes the value at $s=0$ zero for every elementary\n   integrand. More generally, if two elementary integrands agree for all\n   $s\\in[0,T]$ except at finitely many deterministic times, then they agree\n   $(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)$-almost everywhere, since a finite set of times is\n   Lebesgue-null and Tonelli computes\n   $\\mathrm dt\\otimes P(\\{u\\}\\times\\Omega)=0$. All integrands and all\n   integrals below are therefore elements of the quotient spaces of\n   $L^2(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)$ and $L^2(P)$; a claim about a process is a claim\n   about its almost-everywhere class unless a representative is explicitly\n   named, and path statements name the continuous representative.\n\n3. **Deterministic coefficients.** If every $\\xi_k$ is a deterministic real\n   number, $H$ is a deterministic step function on $[0,T]$, so the elementary\n   integrands include all step functions with deterministic coefficients. These\n   are the integrands for which the integral is a Gaussian variable below.\n\nThe Axiom of Choice is declared because the Brownian construction and the\nconditional-expectation interface used in items 6, 7 and 13 assume it; the\ndefinition itself, including the predictability computation of clause 1, uses\nno choice. The countable-choice obligations inherited from that interface are declared\nas dependencies of this item.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the standing hypothesis (H) of\n[[def-elementary-predictable-brownian-integrand]]. Fix a horizon $T>0$ and let\n$\\mathcal P_T$ be the predictable sigma-algebra on $[0,T]\\times\\Omega$\n[[def-progressively-measurable-and-predictable-process]]. Let $H$ be a\npredictable process with\n$$E\\int_0^TH_s^2\\,ds=\\int_{[0,T]\\times\\Omega}H^2\\,d(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)<\\infty ,$$\nso that $H$ is an element of the quotient space\n$L^2(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)$ over $\\mathcal P_T$; its class is what is integrated.\nThe **Ito integral** $\\int_0^TH_s\\,dB_s$, also written $H\\cdot B$ or $I_T(H)$,\nis defined as follows.\n\n1. **Construction.** By the density theorem\n   [[thm-density-of-elementary-predictable-processes-in-predictable-l2]] there\n   is a sequence $(H^n)$ of bounded elementary predictable integrands\n   [[def-elementary-predictable-brownian-integrand]] with\n   $\\|H^n-H\\|_{L^2(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)}\\to0$. For such a sequence the\n   elementary integrals $I_T(H^n)$\n   [[def-ito-integral-of-an-elementary-predictable-process]] form a Cauchy\n   sequence in $L^2(P)$: by the elementary isometry\n   [[thm-ito-isometry-for-elementary-integrands]] and the well-definedness of\n   the elementary integral,\n   $$E\\bigl(I_T(H^n)-I_T(H^m)\\bigr)^2=E\\int_0^T(H^n-H^m)^2ds=\\|H^n-H^m\\|^2_{L^2(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)} ,$$\n   and the right-hand side tends to $0$. Since $L^2(P)$ is complete\n   [[thm-riesz-fischer-completeness-of-l-p]], the classes $I_T(H^n)$ converge\n   in $L^2(P)$; the limit is a class in $L^2(P)$, and\n   $$\\int_0^TH_s\\,dB_s:=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}I_T(H^n)\\qquad\\text{in }L^2(P).$$\n   The limit is a random variable only up to almost-sure equality; statements\n   about it are statements about that class. The limit does not depend on the\n   chosen sequence: if another elementary sequence $K^n\\to H$ is used, then\n   the elementary isometry and linearity give\n   $$\\|I_T(H^n)-I_T(K^n)\\|_{L^2(P)} =\\|H^n-K^n\\|_{L^2(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)} \\le\\|H^n-H\\|_2+\\|K^n-H\\|_2\\longrightarrow0,$$\n   so the two $L^2(P)$ limits agree.\n\n2. **Integrals at a time.** For $t\\in[0,T]$ put\n   $\\int_0^tH_s\\,dB_s:=\\int_0^TH_s1_{[0,t]}(s)\\,dB_s$, the construction of\n   clause 1 applied to the truncated process $H1_{[0,t]}$. That process is\n   predictable: $H$ is $\\mathcal P_T$-measurable and $1_{[0,t]}$ is a\n   deterministic predictable indicator, so the product is\n   $\\mathcal P_T$-measurable; and it is square-integrable because\n   $|H1_{[0,t]}|\\le|H|$ pointwise. The same construction applied to $H$\n   itself gives $t=T$. The family\n   $\\bigl(\\int_0^tH\\,dB\\bigr)_{t\\in[0,T]}$ is a family of $L^2(P)$-classes\n   indexed by $t$; its continuous version is supplied by item 13, and no path\n   property is asserted by this definition.\n\n3. **Consistency with elementary integrands.** If $H$ is itself elementary,\n   then the constant sequence $H^n:=H$ is admissible in clause 1, so\n   $\\int_0^TH\\,dB$ is the limit of the constant sequence of elementary sums,\n   namely the elementary sum $I_T(H)$ of\n   [[def-ito-integral-of-an-elementary-predictable-process]]. The same argument\n   with $H1_{[0,t]}$ in place of $H$, and the finite telescoping of the\n   elementary sum over a refinement containing $t$, gives\n   $\\int_0^tH\\,dB=I_t(H)$ for every $t\\in[0,T]$. In particular the general\n   definition extends, rather than replaces, the elementary definition.\n\n4. **Linearity in the integrand is not asserted here.** Clause 1 defines each\n   integral separately from an arbitrary approximating sequence; the linear and\n   isometric properties of the extension are item 12. Until item 12 is proved,\n   linearity may be used only for elementary integrands, where it is part of\n   [[def-ito-integral-of-an-elementary-predictable-process]].\n\n5. **Comparison with deterministic Riemann integration.** The integral is a\n   stochastic integral: the integrand is paired with the Brownian path through\n   left-endpoint sums and an $L^2(P)$ limit. This definition does not assert\n   convergence of arbitrary tagged Riemann--Stieltjes sums for a general\n   predictable integrand. Particular integrands can have a pathwise\n   interpretation: for $H\\equiv1$ every tagged sum telescopes to $B_T-B_0$,\n   which is also its Ito integral by clause 3 (the value at time zero is\n   irrelevant to its product-measure class). The notation $\\int_0^TH\\,dB$\n   here always refers to the $L^2(P)$-class defined above.\n\nThe Axiom of Choice is declared because the construction selects an\napproximating sequence and uses the completeness and conditional-expectation\ninterfaces that assume it; the inherited obligations are declared as\ndependencies of this item. An alternative construction that avoids selecting the sequence is\nnot needed, because clause 1 shows every sequence gives the same class.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-ito-isometry-and-linearity-in-predictable-l2",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the standing hypothesis (H) of\n[[def-elementary-predictable-brownian-integrand]]. Fix $T>0$. For predictable\n$H,K$ with finite energy $E\\int_0^TH^2ds,E\\int_0^TK^2ds<\\infty$ the integrals\n$\\int_0^TH\\,dB$ of\n[[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]] satisfy\n$$\\int_0^T(H+K)\\,dB=\\int_0^TH\\,dB+\\int_0^TK\\,dB,\\qquad \\int_0^T(aH)\\,dB=a\\int_0^TH\\,dB\\quad(a\\in\\mathbb R),$$\nand the identities are equalities of $L^2(P)$-classes (almost sure\nequalities). Moreover the map $H\\mapsto\\int_0^TH\\,dB$ is an isometry,\n$$E\\Bigl(\\int_0^TH\\,dB\\Bigr)^2=E\\int_0^TH^2ds=\\|H\\|^2_{L^2(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)},$$\nit takes values in the mean-zero subspace of $L^2(P)$, and the bilinear cross\nidentity\n$$E\\Bigl[\\Bigl(\\int_0^TH\\,dB\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\int_0^TK\\,dB\\Bigr)\\Bigr]=E\\int_0^TH_sK_s\\,ds$$\nholds for all such $H,K$. In particular the image of the map is a closed\nsubspace of $L^2(P)$ isometric to the predictable $L^2$ space of $H$'s, and\n$H\\mapsto\\int_0^TH\\,dB$ is injective up to the $(\\mathrm dt\\otimes P)$-class.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-ito-integral-process-has-a-continuous-martingale-version",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the standing hypothesis (H) of\n[[def-elementary-predictable-brownian-integrand]]. Let $H$ be a predictable\nprocess with $E\\int_0^TH_s^2ds<\\infty$ for every finite $T$. Then there is an\nadapted process $M=(M_t)_{t\\ge0}$ with continuous paths such that\n$M_t=\\int_0^tH_s\\,dB_s$ almost surely for every $t\\ge0$\n[[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]], and $M$ is a\nsquare-integrable martingale relative to $(\\mathcal F_t)$\n[[def-continuous-time-adapted-process-and-martingale]]: $EM_t^2=E\\int_0^tH_s^2ds<\\infty$\nfor every $t$. Any two such continuous versions agree at every time on one\nmeasurable event of probability one; in particular the continuous version is\nunique in this common-full-measure-event sense, and it is the only continuous\nsquare-integrable martingale whose value at each deterministic $t$ is the\nintegral class.\n\nThis conclusion is deliberately distinguished from the exact definition of\nindistinguishability in\n[[def-law-modification-and-indistinguishability-of-processes]]. On a\nnoncomplete probability space the full equality set, although it contains the\nmeasurable probability-one event constructed below, need not itself be\nmeasurable. On a complete probability space the two notions coincide, because\nthe complement of the equality set is then a measurable subset of a null set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-deterministic-ito-integrals-are-gaussian",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the standing hypothesis (H) of\n[[def-elementary-predictable-brownian-integrand]]. Fix $T>0$ and let\n$h\\in L^2[0,T]$ be deterministic, choose a Borel representative of its\nLebesgue-equivalence class, and set $h(s,\\omega):=h(s)$. This process is\npredictable with finite energy. Then the Ito integral\n$\\int_0^Th_s\\,dB_s$\n[[def-ito-integral-for-square-integrable-predictable-processes]] is centered\nnormal with variance $\\int_0^Th^2ds$: its law is $N(0,\\int_0^Th^2ds)$ in the\nconvention of [[def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws]], where $N(0,0)$ is the\nDirac law at $0$. More generally, for deterministic $h_1,\\dots,h_d\\in L^2[0,T]$\nthe vector of integrals is jointly Gaussian in the sense of\n[[def-multivariate-normal-law]]: its law is $N_d(0,\\Sigma)$ with\n$$\\Sigma_{jk}=\\int_0^Th_j(s)h_k(s)\\,ds .$$\nIn particular the covariance of the pair is\n$\\operatorname{Cov}(\\int h_jdB,\\int h_kdB)=\\int h_jh_k$, the integrals are\nuncorrelated exactly when $\\int h_jh_k=0$, and integrals of deterministic\nintegrands with disjoint supports are independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.4",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.4",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The complex exponential is entire, and\n\n$$ \\exp'(z)=\\exp z $$\n\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-gaussian-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}\\,dx=\\sqrt\\pi.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.3",
            "2.4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On $[0,T]$, $g(s)=e^{\\gamma s}$ is deterministic, continuous, and square-integrable. Thus $W_t=\\int_0^t g(s)\\,dB_s$ is defined by [F3], and [F4] supplies a continuous version. For a partition $0=t_0<\\cdots<t_n=t$, the deterministic step sums $\\sum_k g(t_k)(B_{t_{k+1}}-B_{t_k})$ converge in $L^2$ to $W_t$ by the Itô isometry and uniform approximation of $g$. Discrete summation by parts rewrites them as $g(t)B_t-g(0)B_0-\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}B_{t_{k+1}}(g(t_{k+1})-g(t_k))$ up to an endpoint term tending to zero. Brownian path continuity makes the last sums converge pathwise to $\\int_0^t g'(s)B_sds$. The $L^2$ limit and this almost-sure limit agree in probability, so $$W_t=e^{\\gamma t}B_t-\\gamma\\int_0^t e^{\\gamma s}B_s\\,ds$$ almost surely for each fixed $t$. Both sides have continuous versions; equality on rational $t$ holds on one probability-one event and therefore extends to all $t\\in[0,T]$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Split the stochastic integral at $s$ and use its linearity: $$X_t=e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}X_s+\\sigma\\int_s^t e^{-\\gamma(t-u)}\\,dB_u.$$ Step-function approximants to the last deterministic-integrand integral depend only on increments after $s$, so [A1] makes them independent of $\\mathcal F_s$. Their $L^2$ limit remains independent of $\\mathcal F_s$: for bounded $\\mathcal F_s$-measurable $Y$ and bounded continuous $\\varphi$, convergence in probability plus boundedness gives convergence of $\\mathbb E[Y\\varphi(\\cdot)]$, preserving the factorization. By [F4] the limit is centered Gaussian; [F3] computes its variance as $\\sigma^2\\int_s^t e^{-2\\gamma(t-u)}du=V(1-e^{-2\\gamma(t-s)})$. This proves the conditional transition law and the Markov property.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Set $X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}X_0+\\sigma e^{-\\gamma t}W_t$. By [F4] it is continuous and adapted. Step 1.1 rewrites it as $$X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}X_0+\\sigma B_t-\\sigma\\gamma\\int_0^t e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}B_s\\,ds.$$ Integrating this continuous expression in $t$, ordinary Fubini [F5] gives $$\\int_0^tX_sds=X_0(1-e^{-\\gamma t})/\\gamma+\\sigma\\int_0^tB_sds-\\sigma\\int_0^tB_u(1-e^{-\\gamma(t-u)})\\,du.$$ Substitution into $X_0-\\gamma\\int_0^tX_sds+\\sigma B_t$ cancels the two $\\int B_sds$ terms and leaves exactly the displayed expression for $X_t$. Thus [F1] holds.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Taking expectations in the explicit solution, the stochastic integral has mean zero by [F4], giving $\\mathbb E X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}\\mathbb E X_0$. The integral is independent of $X_0$ and centered, so its variance adds to $e^{-2\\gamma t}\\operatorname{Var}(X_0)$; [F3] evaluates the added variance as $V(1-e^{-2\\gamma t})$. The independent Gaussian term tends in law to $N(0,V)$ because its variance tends to $V$ and $e^{-\\gamma t}X_0\\to0$ in $L^2$; for any bounded continuous test, restrict to a compact interval, use uniform continuity and the second-moment tail bound, then take the interval radius to infinity.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "Write $r=e^{-\\gamma t}$ and $\\pi(x)=(2\\pi V)^{-1/2}e^{-x^2/(2V)}$. The transition density is $$K_t(x,y)=[2\\pi V(1-r^2)]^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{(y-rx)^2}{2V(1-r^2)}\\right).$$ Multiplying by $\\pi(x)$ gives a symmetric prefactor and exponent proportional to $-[x^2+y^2-2rxy]/[2V(1-r^2)]$, unchanged when $x$ and $y$ are exchanged. Hence $\\pi(x)K_t(x,y)=\\pi(y)K_t(y,x)$, the detailed-balance identity.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-4",
          "claim": "For $t>0$ let $\\mu=e^{-\\gamma t}x$, $v=V(1-e^{-2\\gamma t})$, and $p(t,x,y)$ be the normal density with mean $\\mu$ and variance $v$. Direct differentiation gives $$\\partial_t p=p\\left(\\frac{\\mu'(y-\\mu)}v+\\frac{v'}2\\left[\\frac{(y-\\mu)^2}{v^2}-\\frac1v\\right]\\right),\\quad \\mu'=-\\gamma\\mu,\\quad v'=\\sigma^2-2\\gamma v.$$ Also direct $y$-differentiation gives $\\gamma\\partial_y(yp)+(\\sigma^2/2)\\partial_{yy}p$ equal to the same expression after writing $y=\\mu+(y-\\mu)$. Thus $\\partial_t p=\\gamma\\partial_y(yp)+(\\sigma^2/2)\\partial_{yy}p$. The Gaussian formula has total integral one and converges to point mass at $x$ as $t\\downarrow0$; this is the stated positive-time Fokker–Planck solution.",
          "step": "2.4",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $X$ and $\\widetilde X$ are two continuous solutions with the same data, their difference $z$ satisfies $z(t)=-\\gamma\\int_0^tz(s)ds$. Let $D(t)=\\int_0^t|z(s)|ds$. Then $D(0)=0$, $D\\ge0$, and $D'(t)=|z(t)|\\le\\gamma D(t)$ by the equation. The derivative of $e^{-\\gamma t}D(t)$ is nonpositive, so this nonnegative function is at most its initial value zero. Hence $D=0$ and continuity gives $z=0$ at every time.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "If $X_0$ is centered normal with variance $V$, step 1.2 gives centered Gaussian finite-dimensional laws, since each stochastic integral over a disjoint increment interval is an independent Gaussian and $X_0$ is independent of future increments. Its one-time variance is $V$. For $s\\le t$, the split formula in 2.1 and independence of the later increment yield $\\mathbb E[X_sX_t]=e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}\\mathbb E[X_s^2]=Ve^{-\\gamma(t-s)}$. Thus covariance depends only on $|t-s|$. A centered Gaussian finite-dimensional law is determined by its covariance matrix; shifting every time by the same amount preserves that matrix and proves stationarity.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $R>0$, the derivative and componentwise FTC in [F6] give $\\int_0^R e^{-(\\gamma-i\\omega)t}dt=(1-e^{-(\\gamma-i\\omega)R})/(\\gamma-i\\omega)$. The modulus formula gives $|e^{-(\\gamma-i\\omega)R}|=e^{-\\gamma R}\\to0$ and $|e^{-(\\gamma-i\\omega)t}|=e^{-\\gamma t}$, whose integral is finite. Thus the stationary covariance is integrable. By evenness, $$S(\\omega)=\\frac V\\pi\\operatorname{Re}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(\\gamma-i\\omega)t}dt =\\frac V\\pi\\operatorname{Re}\\frac1{\\gamma-i\\omega} =\\frac{V\\gamma}{\\pi(\\gamma^2+\\omega^2)}.$$ The integral converges absolutely because $\\gamma>0$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F6",
            "step 3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For the stationary time average, Fubini applies to the random product because $\\mathbb E|X_sX_t|\\le(\\mathbb E X_s^2\\mathbb E X_t^2)^{1/2}=V$, so its absolute integral over the finite square and probability space is at most $VT^2$. Changing variables in the two triangular halves gives $$\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(T^{-1}\\int_0^TX_tdt\\right) =\\frac{2V}{T^2}\\int_0^T(T-s)e^{-\\gamma s}ds \\le\\frac{2V}{\\gamma T}.$$ The bound tends to zero, so these averages converge to zero in $L^2$ and in probability. It asserts no almost-sure theorem for arbitrary observables.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F5",
            "step 3.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement fixes positive gamma and sigma and a finite interval.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Assume the filtered probability space, Brownian increment hypothesis, and initial data of [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]], including $\\mathbb E X_0^2<\\infty$ and future increments independent of $\\mathcal F_0$. For $\\gamma,\\sigma>0$, on every finite time interval the process\n$$X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}X_0+\\sigma e^{-\\gamma t}\\int_0^t e^{\\gamma s}\\,dB_s$$\nhas a continuous adapted version, solves the stated Ornstein–Uhlenbeck integral equation, and is the unique continuous solution driven by the same $(X_0,B)$. Put $V=\\sigma^2/(2\\gamma)$. For $0\\le s<t$, its transition law conditional on $\\mathcal F_s$ is normal with mean $e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}X_s$ and variance $V(1-e^{-2\\gamma(t-s)})$. Consequently\n$$\\mathbb E X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}\\mathbb E X_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=e^{-2\\gamma t}\\operatorname{Var}(X_0)+V(1-e^{-2\\gamma t}).$$\nFrom any such initial law, $X_t$ converges in distribution to $N(0,V)$. If $X_0\\sim N(0,V)$ and is independent of future Brownian increments, the process is stationary Gaussian with covariance $C(\\tau)=Ve^{-\\gamma|\\tau|}$, and its transition kernel satisfies detailed balance with the $N(0,V)$ density. Its transition density solves the Fokker–Planck equation for positive time. Under the convention $S(\\omega)=(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_{\\mathbb R}C(\\tau)e^{i\\omega\\tau}\\,d\\tau$, the stationary spectrum and time-average bound are\n$$S(\\omega)=\\frac{V\\gamma}{\\pi(\\gamma^2+\\omega^2)},\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac1T\\int_0^T X_t\\,dt\\right)\\le\\frac{2V}{\\gamma T}.$$\nAll these statements concern the specified linear stochastic model. An Einstein relation between $\\sigma$ and a physical bath temperature would be an additional physical postulate.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "bac5d138f21f85ee6c80d0762d81e1fc5996d52cb4cd50b5771c6d733037a6bd",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true,
            "case_interpretation": "Gamma and sigma are strictly positive; finite horizon T>0 and supplied probability/Brownian data exclude an empty model."
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: The time-zero initial value and zero-mean noise terms are included .",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Assume the Axiom of Choice [[def-axiom-of-choice]]. Assume the filtered probability space, Brownian increment hypothesis, and initial data of [[def-tdmath-ornstein-uhlenbeck-equation]], including $\\mathbb E X_0^2<\\infty$ and future increments independent of $\\mathcal F_0$. For $\\gamma,\\sigma>0$, on every finite time interval the process\n$$X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}X_0+\\sigma e^{-\\gamma t}\\int_0^t e^{\\gamma s}\\,dB_s$$\nhas a continuous adapted version, solves the stated Ornstein–Uhlenbeck integral equation, and is the unique continuous solution driven by the same $(X_0,B)$. Put $V=\\sigma^2/(2\\gamma)$. For $0\\le s<t$, its transition law conditional on $\\mathcal F_s$ is normal with mean $e^{-\\gamma(t-s)}X_s$ and variance $V(1-e^{-2\\gamma(t-s)})$. Consequently\n$$\\mathbb E X_t=e^{-\\gamma t}\\mathbb E X_0,\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)=e^{-2\\gamma t}\\operatorname{Var}(X_0)+V(1-e^{-2\\gamma t}).$$\nFrom any such initial law, $X_t$ converges in distribution to $N(0,V)$. If $X_0\\sim N(0,V)$ and is independent of future Brownian increments, the process is stationary Gaussian with covariance $C(\\tau)=Ve^{-\\gamma|\\tau|}$, and its transition kernel satisfies detailed balance with the $N(0,V)$ density. Its transition density solves the Fokker–Planck equation for positive time. Under the convention $S(\\omega)=(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_{\\mathbb R}C(\\tau)e^{i\\omega\\tau}\\,d\\tau$, the stationary spectrum and time-average bound are\n$$S(\\omega)=\\frac{V\\gamma}{\\pi(\\gamma^2+\\omega^2)},\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\frac1T\\int_0^T X_t\\,dt\\right)\\le\\frac{2V}{\\gamma T}.$$\nAll these statements concern the specified linear stochastic model. An Einstein relation between $\\sigma$ and a physical bath temperature would be an additional physical postulate.",
