        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: A zero nonnegative production integral forces A=B almost everywhere by the level-set argument ."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "For g(v)=alpha+b dot v-c|v|^2 with c>0, elastic momentum and energy conservation make g(v)+g(w)=g(v prime)+g(w prime), so A=B and the production is zero.",
          "evidence": "Substitute into the collision-invariant calculation in steps 2.2–2.4."
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-elastic-collision-kernel.md",
          "status": "Current draft mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected, acceptance not inferred.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $d\\ge2$. The **elastic collision set** is\n$$Q_{\\rm el}:=\\{(v,w,v',w')\\in\\mathbb R^{4d}:v+w=v'+w',\\ |v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2\\},$$\nwith its relative Borel sigma-algebra. A collision measure is a nonnegative countably additive measure $\\nu$ on this measurable space. It is symmetric if it is invariant under the exchanges $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(w,v)$, $(v',w')\\leftrightarrow(w',v')$, and $(v,w)\\leftrightarrow(v',w')$. These invariances are hypotheses on $\\nu$, not consequences of energy and momentum conservation.\n\nFor a positive function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to(0,\\infty)$, write $Q(f)$ for a collision operator when the following **weak collision identity** holds for each test function $\\varphi$ used:\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}\\varphi(v)Q(f)(v)\\,dv=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(f(v')f(w')-f(v)f(w))(\\varphi(v)+\\varphi(w)-\\varphi(v')-\\varphi(w'))\\,d\\nu,$$\nwith absolute convergence. This identity is the adopted mathematical collision model. Molecular chaos or a microscopic derivation of it is not part of the definition.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F1 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 at 2.1, 4.1, 7.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.\n\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F2 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 at 1.1, 2.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. For $E \\in \\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$, define\n\n$$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(E) := \\int_X \\nu(E_x)\\,d\\mu = \\int_Y \\mu(E^y)\\,d\\nu.$$\n\nThe two displayed integrals are well-defined by\n[[prop-sigma-finite-section-measure-functions-are-measurable]] and equal by\n[[thm-iterated-section-measures-agree-on-product-measurable-sets]]. This is the\n**product measure** of $\\mu$ and $\\nu$.\n",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F2 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 at 1.1, 2.1, 3.1"
        },
        {
