          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F1 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 at 2.1, 2.2, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-regular-energy-shell.md",
          "status": "Current draft mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected, acceptance not inferred.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $O\\subset\\mathbb R^m$, $m\\ge2$, be open, let $H\\in C^2(O,\\mathbb R)$, and let $J\\subset\\mathbb R$ be open. An energy $E\\in J$ is **regular** if $\\nabla H(x)\\ne0$ for every $x\\in\\Sigma_E:=H^{-1}(\\{E\\})$. By the implicit-function theorem, a neighborhood of each point of a nonempty regular level is a graph $x_i=h(z)$.\n\nIn a graph chart, use the local area density $\\sqrt{1+|\\nabla h(z)|^2}\\,dz$, where $dz$ is $(m-1)$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. A **graph partition** on $\\Sigma_E$ is a locally finite graph-chart cover with nonnegative smooth functions $\\chi_a$ supported in their respective charts and summing to one on the level. Whenever such a partition is supplied, define the candidate weighted shell integral by\n$$\\mu_E(g):=\\sum_a\\int g(z,h_a(z))\\chi_a(z,h_a(z))|\\partial_iH(z,h_a(z))|^{-1}\\,dz$$\nfor nonnegative Borel $g$. Its independence from the chosen chart partition, and its equality with $\\int_{\\Sigma_E}g|\\nabla H|^{-1}dA_E$, are proved in the theorem on this page. If $0<\\mu_E(1)<\\infty$, the **microcanonical shell probability** is $\\widehat\\mu_E:=\\mu_E/\\mu_E(1)$.\n\nThis is an explicit chart-based definition, not a formal delta distribution $\\delta(H-E)$. Critical levels are excluded.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F1 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 at 2.1, 2.2, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem",
          "carrier": "items/thm-euclidean-implicit-function-theorem.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $m,n\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{m+n}$ be open, and let\n$F:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be $C^1$.  Suppose $(a,b)\\in U$, $F(a,b)=0$, and the\n**partial derivative in the second block**\n\n$$D_yF(a,b):\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n,\\qquad D_yF(a,b)v:=DF(a,b)(0,v),$$\n\nis invertible.  Put similarly $D_xF(a,b)u:=DF(a,b)(u,0)$.  Then there are open\nneighbourhoods $P$ of $a$ and $Q$ of $b$, and a unique $C^1$ map\n$\\varphi:P\\to Q$, such that\n\n$$F(x,y)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y=\\varphi(x)\\qquad((x,y)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nAfter shrinking $P,Q$ if necessary, $D_yF(x,\\varphi(x))$ is invertible and\n\n$$D\\varphi(x)=-D_yF(x,\\varphi(x))^{-1}D_xF(x,\\varphi(x)).$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F2 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 at 1.1, 2.1, 2.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions",
          "carrier": "items/thm-c-one-change-of-variables-for-nonnegative-lebesgue-measurable-functions.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$U,V \\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $T : U \\to V$ be a\n$C^1$ diffeomorphism. For every nonnegative Lebesgue measurable\n$f : V \\to [0,\\infty]$,\n$$ \\int_V f(y)\\,d\\lambda_n(y) = \\int_U f(T(x))\\,|\\det DT(x)|\\,d\\lambda_n(x). $$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F3 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 at 1.2, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to [0,\\infty]$ be product-measurable. Then\n$x \\mapsto \\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu$ and $y \\mapsto \\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu$ are measurable,\nand\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F4 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 at 1.1, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra",
          "carrier": "items/def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix $n \\ge 1$ and let $\\lambda_n^*$ be the Lebesgue outer set function on $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n([[def-lebesgue-outer-measure]]). A set $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n**Lebesgue measurable** when\n\n$$\\lambda_n^*(A) \\;=\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\cap E) \\;+\\; \\lambda_n^*(A \\setminus E) \\qquad \\text{for every } A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n .$$\n\nThis formula makes sense before any outer-measure theorem is invoked. Under\ncountable choice, [[thm-lebesgue-outer-measure-is-an-outer-measure-agreeing-with-volume]]\nmakes $\\lambda_n^*$ an outer measure, and the displayed condition is then exactly\nCarathéodory measurability in the sense of [[def-caratheodory-measurable-set]].\n\nThe family of Lebesgue measurable sets is written $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$,\nand **Lebesgue measure** is the restriction\n\n$$\\lambda_n \\;:=\\; \\lambda_n^*\\!\\restriction_{\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)} .$$\n\nOn the real line the subscript is dropped and $\\lambda := \\lambda_1$.\n\nThe quantifier over every test set $A$ is part of the condition, and no\nhypothesis on $E$ is imposed before it is tested. That\n$\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$ is a sigma-algebra and that $\\lambda_n$ is a complete\nmeasure on it are not part of this definition: they are proved, under the Axiom\nof Countable Choice, in [[thm-lebesgue-measure-is-a-complete-measure]], which is\nrecorded in this item's `justified_by` because it is a statement about the\nobjects introduced here. Until that theorem the symbols $\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbb{R}^n)$\nand $\\lambda_n$ name a family of sets and a restricted set function, nothing more.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F4 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 at 1.1, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-dominated-convergence.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F6 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 at 3.1, 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-distance-to-set-is-lipschitz",
          "carrier": "items/lem-distance-to-set-is-lipschitz.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]), let $A \\subseteq X$ be\nnonempty and let $x, y \\in X$. Then\n\n$$|d(x,A) - d(y,A)| \\le d(x,y),$$\n\nwith $d(\\cdot,A)$ the distance to a nonempty set ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\nThus the real-valued function $u \\mapsto d(u,A)$ changes by at most $d(u,v)$\nbetween $u$ and $v$: it is **$1$-Lipschitz**.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F7 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 at 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-metric-closure-characterisation",
          "carrier": "items/thm-metric-closure-characterisation.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $A \\subseteq X$,\nwith closure, derived set and limit points as in\n[[def-metric-interior-closure-boundary]]. Then:\n\n1. If $A \\ne \\emptyset$, then $\\overline{A} = \\{\\, x \\in X : d(x,A) = 0 \\,\\}$,\n   where $d(x,A)$ is the distance from a point to a nonempty set\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n2. $\\overline{A} = A \\cup A'$.\n3. $\\overline{A}$ is closed, contains $A$, and is contained in every closed\n   $F \\subseteq X$ with $A \\subseteq F$. So $\\overline{A}$ is the smallest closed\n   superset of $A$, and $A$ is closed if and only if $A = \\overline{A}$.\n\nClaims 2 and 3 hold for every $A$, the empty set included: $\\overline{\\emptyset}$\nis empty because no ball meets $\\emptyset$, and $\\emptyset$ is closed because $X$\nis open ([[def-metric-topology]]). Claim 1 carries the hypothesis $A \\ne \\emptyset$\nbecause $d(x,A)$ is defined only for nonempty $A$\n([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F7 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 at 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-finite-measure-uniqueness-on-a-pi-system",
          "carrier": "items/lem-finite-measure-uniqueness-on-a-pi-system.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\mathcal P$ be a pi-system on $X$ and let $\\mathcal A=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal P)$. If finite measures $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ on $(X,\\mathcal A)$ agree on $\\mathcal P$ and satisfy $\\mu(X)=\\nu(X)$, then $\\mu=\\nu$ on $\\mathcal A$.\n\nThe total-mass equality is separate because this library's pi-system convention does not require $X\\in\\mathcal P$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F8 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result with proof present; this local consumer review does not certify its entire transitive closure.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 at 5.1"
        }
