      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy",
          "carrier": "items/def-tdmath-mixture-generator-space-and-energy.md",
          "status": "Current draft mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected, acceptance not inferred.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $K$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with its Borel sigma-algebra, and let $a:K\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. The **convex hull** $\\operatorname{conv}K$ is the set of finite convex combinations of points of $K$. A **phase generator** is a point $y\\in K$ with declared energy $a(y)$. A **mixture** is a Borel probability measure $P$ on $K$. Its barycenter and mean energy are the coordinatewise Lebesgue integrals\n$$\\bar y(P):=\\int_K y\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R^d,\\qquad \\bar a(P):=\\int_K a(y)\\,dP(y)\\in\\mathbb R.$$\nThese integrals are finite because each coordinate and $a$ are bounded on compact $K$. For prescribed mean $x\\in\\operatorname{conv}K$, the **mixture energy** is\n$$a_{\\rm mix}(x):=\\inf\\{\\bar a(P):P\\text{ is a Borel probability on }K,\\ \\bar y(P)=x\\}.$$\nThe admissible set is nonempty since every finite convex combination of points of $K$ is the barycenter of a finite atomic probability. The definition does not assert that the infimum is attained; the theorem on this page gives a compact continuous sufficient condition.\n\n",
