          "status": "Current draft mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected, acceptance not inferred.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $(M,\\omega)$ be a smooth $2n$-dimensional symplectic manifold and $H\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$, with Hamiltonian field defined by $\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH$. Wherever its local flow $\\phi_t$ is defined, it preserves the symplectic form and the volume measure $m$ determined by $\\omega^n/n!$. No completeness is asserted. Let $R$ be a measurable region invariant under a nonzero-time map $T=\\phi_\\tau$, so $T$ restricts to a self-map $R\\to R$; then this restriction preserves the volume measure $m|_R$. If $m(R)<\\infty$, then for every measurable $E\\subseteq R$, almost every point of $E$ returns to $E$ infinitely often under forward iteration.\n\nIf $m(R)=1$ and $T$ is ergodic, then for every $f\\in L^1(R,m)$ the averages $N^{-1}\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}f(T^jx)$ converge almost everywhere and in $L^1$ to $\\int_Rf\\,dm$. Without ergodicity, the limit is the invariant conditional expectation supplied by Birkhoff's theorem, not necessarily the space mean. Finally, if each stated time map $\\phi_t$ is a diffeomorphism of a finite invariant region $R$, a density $p_0\\ge0$ with $\\int_Rp_0\\,dm=1$ and $\\int_R|p_0\\log p_0|\\,dm<\\infty$ transports by $p_t=p_0\\circ\\phi_{-t}$ with conserved fine-grained entropy $-k_B\\int_Rp_t\\log p_t\\,dm$, using $0\\log0=0$. Volume preservation alone does not imply ergodicity or a time-average/ensemble identity.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F2 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Draft proof present; no production acceptance asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 at 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system",
          "carrier": "items/def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system.md",
          "status": "Published read-only mathematical supplier; exact current interface inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A measure-preserving system is **ergodic for $\\mu$** if each $E\\in\\mathcal I$ has $\\mu(E)=0$ or $\\mu(X\\setminus E)=0$, with $\\mathcal I$ as in [[def-strict-and-mod-null-invariant-sigma-algebras]]. For a probability system this means $\\mu(E)\\in\\{0,1\\}$. The definition is relative to the invariant measure; no probability assumption is implicit in the general null/conull formulation.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Use is limited to the exact conditions recorded in F3 and the item risk review; no stronger regularity or unqualified physical conclusion is inferred.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition; any well-definedness discharges retain their actual supplier status.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 at 2.1"
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-12",
        "notes": "Checked both steps and all four direct suppliers, including repaired recurrence theorem. With iota_X omega=dH, H=cos q gives X=sin q partial_p, hence phi_t(q,p)=(q,p+t sin q) modulo 2pi, with inverse and all real times. Full torus is invariant of finite area 4pi^2; event cos q>0 has area 2pi^2 and is invariant at every sampling time. Thus normalized measure is nonergodic despite volume conservation. Zero boundary cos q=0 contributes no area; AC convention inherited from recurrence supplier. No empirical inference."
      }
    },
    "ex-tdmath-pure-state-versus-average-entropy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The complex exponential is entire, and\n\n$$ \\exp'(z)=\\exp z $$\n\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathbb F$ be $\\mathbb R$ or $\\mathbb C$, with **conjugation** $z\\mapsto\\overline z$\nthe identity on $\\mathbb R$ and complex conjugation on $\\mathbb C$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]). A **real** or\n**complex inner-product space** is an $\\mathbb F$-vector space $V$ together with\nan inner product $\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle:V\\times V\\to\\mathbb F$ in the sense\nof [[def-inner-product-space]]: for all $u,v,w\\in V$ and $a,b\\in\\mathbb F$,\n\n$$\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle=a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle v,u\\rangle},\\qquad \\langle v,v\\rangle\\ge0,\\quad \\langle v,v\\rangle=0\\iff v=0 .$$\n\n**Conjugate-linearity in the second argument.** Conjugate symmetry together with\nlinearity in the first argument gives, for all $w\\in V$,\n\n$$\\langle w,au+bv\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle au+bv,w\\rangle}=\\overline{a\\langle u,w\\rangle+b\\langle v,w\\rangle}=\\overline a\\,\\langle w,u\\rangle+\\overline b\\,\\langle w,v\\rangle .$$\n\n**Induced length.** The **induced length**, or inner-product norm, of $v$ is\n\n$$\\|v\\|:=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is exactly the published [[def-inner-product-norm]]: positive definiteness\nmakes $\\langle v,v\\rangle$ a nonnegative real with a unique nonnegative square\nroot, so that $\\|v\\|\\ge0$, $\\|v\\|=0$ exactly for $v=0$, and\n$\\langle v,v\\rangle=\\|v\\|^2$.\n\n**The convention on this page.** Every inner-product space below is real or\ncomplex in this sense, the pairing is linear in the first argument and\nconjugate-linear in the second, and $\\|v\\|$ always denotes the induced length.\nIn the real case conjugation is the identity and $|z|$ is the absolute value of\n$\\mathbb R$; in the complex case they are complex conjugation and the complex\nmodulus. All statements below are therefore written once and read in either\nscalar field.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "proof-step-1-1",
          "claim": "The unitary evolution sends $\\psi$ to $(e^{-iE_1t/\\hbar}u_1+e^{-iE_2t/\\hbar}u_2)/\\sqrt2$, which remains a unit vector by [F2]. Its rank-one density has eigenvalues $1,0$, so its entropy is $-k_B(1\\log1+0\\log0)=0$ for every $t$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-2-1",
          "claim": "The two energy eigenspaces are one-dimensional. The matrix entry between them is an exponential with nonzero frequency; by [F1] its average has magnitude at most $\\hbar/(T|E_1-E_2|)$ and tends to zero. The diagonal entries remain $1/2$, so the direct finite-dimensional average yields $\\rho_{\\rm diag}=\\tfrac12P_1+\\tfrac12P_2$. Its eigenvalues are $(1/2,1/2)$, so $$S(\\rho_{\\rm diag})=-k_B\\left(\\tfrac12\\log\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\log\\tfrac12\\right)=k_B\\log2.$$ Since every $S(\\rho_t)=0$, the time average of instantaneous entropy is exactly zero.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "proof-step-3-1",
          "claim": "In the basis $(u_1,u_2)$, $\\bar\\rho_T$ has diagonal entries $1/2$ and off-diagonal entry $$c_T=\\frac1{2T}\\int_0^T e^{-i(E_1-E_2)t/\\hbar}\\,dt,$$ whose magnitude is at most $\\hbar/(T|E_1-E_2|)$ by direct integration. Its eigenvalues are $1/2\\pm|c_T|$, so both tend to $1/2$. Continuity of $x\\mapsto-x\\log x$ at $1/2$ gives $S(\\bar\\rho_T)\\to k_B\\log2$. Thus entropy of the averaged state has a nonzero limit even though the averaged instantaneous entropy remains zero.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
